A. Importing Libraries
- Pandas is used to load, filter, group, and analyze air quality data.
- Matplotlib is used to generate visualizations.

B. Loading and Exploring the Dataset
- read_csv() loads the CSV file.
- parse_dates=["Date"] instructs Pandas to parse the Date column as dates during loading.
- head() displays the first five rows.
- info() displays column names, data types, and non-null counts.

C. Selecting One City
- df["City"] == "Delhi" creates a Boolean condition.
- df[condition] selects rows where the city is Delhi.
- sort_values("Date") arranges records in chronological order.

D. Extracting the Year
- dt.year extracts the year from each date.
- The extracted value is stored in a new column called Year.
- This makes year-wise grouping possible.

E. AQI Trend Line Plot
- plot() creates a graph from the selected DataFrame columns.
- x="Date" places dates on the horizontal axis.
- y="AQI" places AQI values on the vertical axis.
- color="red" sets the line color.
- title gives the graph a heading.
- ylabel() labels the vertical axis.
- show() displays the graph.

F. Pollutant Trend Subplots
- y=["PM2.5", "PM10", "CO"] selects three pollutant columns.
- subplots=True creates a separate plot for each pollutant.
- figsize=(10, 7) controls the overall figure size.
- Separate plots make the pollutant trends easier to inspect.

G. Yearly Average AQI Bar Plot
- groupby("Year") groups records by year.
- ["AQI"].mean() calculates the average AQI for each year.
- kind="bar" creates a bar chart.
- The chart compares average AQI across years.

H. Stacked Bar Chart of AQI Categories
- pd.crosstab() creates a frequency table between Year and AQI_Bucket.
- Each cell represents the number of records for a category in a year.
- kind="bar" creates bars.
- stacked=True stacks the category counts within each yearly bar.
- The y-axis is labelled Days; this represents days only if the dataset has one record per day.

I. Box Plots
- df[["PM2.5", "PM10", "CO"]] selects the pollutant columns.
- kind="box" creates box plots for their distributions.
- boxplot(column="AQI", by="Year") compares AQI distributions across years.
- suptitle("") removes the automatically generated overall title.

J. Scatter Plot
- kind="scatter" creates a scatter plot.
- PM2.5 is shown on the x-axis and AQI on the y-axis.
- Each point represents one observation.
- The graph helps inspect the relationship between PM2.5 and AQI.

K. Bubble Chart
- dropna(subset=[...]) removes rows missing PM10, AQI, or CO.
- PM10 is shown on the x-axis and AQI on the y-axis.
- s=d["CO"] * 10 sets bubble size based on CO values.
- alpha=0.4 makes bubbles partly transparent.
- Larger bubbles represent higher CO values according to the selected scaling.

